[{:db/id 17592186203893 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f60f67b-c6e2-55b6-a159-dee4ac2e0060" "question-2c11ba83-6848-5a89-900b-5fb2ae872fa8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203680 :question/worked-solution "Since the modulus $n=5$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x = 0,1,2,3,4$ into the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 2\\cdot 1 & ≡2≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & ≡4≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 2\\cdot 3 & ≡6≡5+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & ✓ \\\\ 2\\cdot 4 & ≡8≡5+3≡3≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is is $x\\equiv {\\color{blue}{3}} \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $3$ modulo $5$ is a solution. For example, note that $8\\equiv 3,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ≡2\\cdot 8\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡16\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 5+1\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡0+1\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡1\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203894 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2b8f3c46-66e9-51ef-a1f9-2699e1171085"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203681 :question/problem "Solve the modular equation $7x \\equiv 3 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:15).$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$  \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=15,$ so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,15)=1.\n$$\n\nThis means our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15).$ To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{2} & ≡49≡3\\cdot 15+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {7}^{3} & ≡4\\cdot 7≡28≡15+13≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {7}^{4} & ≡13\\cdot 7≡91≡6\\cdot 15+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, $13 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15),$ which means that $13$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $15$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $13,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 13\\cdot 7x & ≡13\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ (13\\cdot 7)x & ≡39 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡2\\cdot 15+9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\equiv 9 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:15).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203895 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3db815a9-d784-52dd-bf7e-2dc644a57255" "question-7b2d85f9-70d5-5d8a-aa2a-9b3b30df44ef"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203705 :question/problem "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n7x - 11 \\equiv 29 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:19)\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax\\equiv b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-11 & ≡29\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x-11+11 & ≡29+11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡40\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡38+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2\\cdot 19+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=19$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,19)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19).$ To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{2} & ≡49≡2\\cdot 19+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ {7}^{3} & ≡11\\cdot 7≡77≡4\\cdot 19+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $11 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:19),$ which means that $11$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $19$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $11,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 11\\cdot 7x & ≡11\\cdot 2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ (11\\cdot 7)x & ≡22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡19+3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 3 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:19).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203896 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3b5eee82-5740-5d92-bedf-cc94230c0e60" "question-6fe75766-c260-5668-9726-1bdf3c8f2f17"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203682 :question/problem "Solve the congruence $15x \\equiv 17 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:44).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$  \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=44,$ where \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,44)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44).$ To find the inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44),$ we use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}44 & = & 15\\cdot 2 & + & 14 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 14\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =44-15\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =15-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =15-14 \\\\  & =15-(44-15\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $44,$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot 3-44 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ 15\\cdot 3 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $15 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:44),$ which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $15$ (modulo $44$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ 3\\cdot 15x & ≡3\\cdot 17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ (3\\cdot 15)x & ≡51 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡44+7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ x & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 7 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:44).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f60f67b-c6e2-55b6-a159-dee4ac2e0060" :question/id #uuid "4f60f67b-c6e2-55b6-a159-dee4ac2e0060" :question/math-academy-id "q-214738" :question/problem "Solve the linear congruence $3x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ using trial and error. Express your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $7.$\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" :question/worked-solution "Since the modulus $n=7$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x=0,1,2,3,4,5,6$ into the congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 1 & ≡3≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 & ≡6≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & ≡9≡7+2≡2≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 4 & ≡12≡7+5≡5≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 5 & ≡15≡2\\cdot 7+1≡1≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 6 & ≡18≡2\\cdot 7+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $6$ modulo $7$ is a solution. For example, note that $13≡6,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ≡3\\cdot 13\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡39\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5\\cdot 7+4\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0+4\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2f9f0ad8-9183-59f0-8835-5839bbca3f42"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2f9f0ad8-9183-59f0-8835-5839bbca3f42" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9f0ad8-9183-59f0-8835-5839bbca3f42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-424edbb9-4ced-5f21-bbeb-ac5e675871e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-424edbb9-4ced-5f21-bbeb-ac5e675871e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-424edbb9-4ced-5f21-bbeb-ac5e675871e7" :answer/id #uuid "424edbb9-4ced-5f21-bbeb-ac5e675871e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2c11ba83-6848-5a89-900b-5fb2ae872fa8" :question/id #uuid "2c11ba83-6848-5a89-900b-5fb2ae872fa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-214736" :question/problem "Solve the linear congruence $5x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ using trial and error. Express your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $6.$\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$" :question/worked-solution "Since the modulus $n=6$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x=0,1,2,3,4,5$ into the congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 1 & ≡5≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & ≡10≡6+4≡4≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 3 & ≡15≡2\\cdot 6+3≡3≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 4 & ≡20≡3\\cdot 6+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & ✓ \\\\ 5\\cdot 5 & ≡25≡4\\cdot 6+1≡1≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $4$ modulo $6$ is a solution. For example, note that $10≡4,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡5\\cdot 10\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡50\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 6+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d2633ee-1d5b-5783-bb62-c0caed17ffa5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d2633ee-1d5b-5783-bb62-c0caed17ffa5" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2633ee-1d5b-5783-bb62-c0caed17ffa5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3bbe80cc-77d0-5bec-b9a3-584c08cefb71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3bbe80cc-77d0-5bec-b9a3-584c08cefb71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bbe80cc-77d0-5bec-b9a3-584c08cefb71" :answer/id #uuid "3bbe80cc-77d0-5bec-b9a3-584c08cefb71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b8f3c46-66e9-51ef-a1f9-2699e1171085" :question/id #uuid "2b8f3c46-66e9-51ef-a1f9-2699e1171085" :question/math-academy-id "q-132139" :question/problem "Solve the modular equation $4x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=4$ and $n=11$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,11)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ To find the inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$, we compute the exponents of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡5\\cdot 4≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{4} & ≡9\\cdot 4≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11),$ which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $11$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 3\\cdot 4x & ≡3\\cdot 7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ (3\\cdot 4)x & ≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡1\\cdot 11+10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a6233de2-e84d-5538-a9c5-3e6781767893"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a6233de2-e84d-5538-a9c5-3e6781767893" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6233de2-e84d-5538-a9c5-3e6781767893" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e2f78442-1c48-529e-80f1-950ea1923966" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e2f78442-1c48-529e-80f1-950ea1923966" "answer-3d34f638-9a0e-5ca4-a67d-6e32e086b9f1" "answer-f740ef79-86a1-5132-98b5-754715a1cf95" "answer-680844b9-8e08-5d3b-87b4-0ea3de54ee20" "answer-b95b1927-2dd5-58b1-bd34-01d6ec3c0908"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2f78442-1c48-529e-80f1-950ea1923966" :answer/id #uuid "e2f78442-1c48-529e-80f1-950ea1923966" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d34f638-9a0e-5ca4-a67d-6e32e086b9f1" :answer/id #uuid "3d34f638-9a0e-5ca4-a67d-6e32e086b9f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f740ef79-86a1-5132-98b5-754715a1cf95" :answer/id #uuid "f740ef79-86a1-5132-98b5-754715a1cf95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-680844b9-8e08-5d3b-87b4-0ea3de54ee20" :answer/id #uuid "680844b9-8e08-5d3b-87b4-0ea3de54ee20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b95b1927-2dd5-58b1-bd34-01d6ec3c0908" :answer/id #uuid "b95b1927-2dd5-58b1-bd34-01d6ec3c0908" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204273 :question/problem "Fill in the missing values, and hence solve the modular equation $6x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$ Express $x$ as a nonnegative integer smaller than $25.$\n\n${6}^{2}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${6}^{3}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${6}^{4}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n${6}^{5}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-4}}\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-5}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=6$ and $n=25$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(6,25)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$ To find the inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$, we compute the exponents of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2} & ≡36≡25+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{3} & ≡11\\cdot 6≡66≡2\\cdot 25+16≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{4} & ≡16\\cdot 6≡96≡3\\cdot 25+21≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{5} & ≡21\\cdot 6≡126≡5\\cdot 25+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $21\\cdot 6≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25),$ which means that $21$ is the multiplicative inverse of $6$ (modulo $25$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $21,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 21\\cdot 6x & ≡21\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ (21\\cdot 6)x & ≡63 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡2\\cdot 25+13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ x & ≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186200417 :answer-field/correct "answer-ad76e3b5-29b9-5bea-a8d9-e0706604d1bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad76e3b5-29b9-5bea-a8d9-e0706604d1bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad76e3b5-29b9-5bea-a8d9-e0706604d1bc" :answer/id #uuid "ad76e3b5-29b9-5bea-a8d9-e0706604d1bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186200415 :answer-field/correct "answer-a41993f1-642c-526e-83fb-2308acfe075f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a41993f1-642c-526e-83fb-2308acfe075f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a41993f1-642c-526e-83fb-2308acfe075f" :answer/id #uuid "a41993f1-642c-526e-83fb-2308acfe075f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186200413 :answer-field/correct "answer-45591275-4f92-5c6a-95be-4ec70c990168" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45591275-4f92-5c6a-95be-4ec70c990168"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45591275-4f92-5c6a-95be-4ec70c990168" :answer/id #uuid "45591275-4f92-5c6a-95be-4ec70c990168" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186200414 :answer-field/correct "answer-23ebcbf3-35d1-5715-bd12-20face8731a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-23ebcbf3-35d1-5715-bd12-20face8731a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23ebcbf3-35d1-5715-bd12-20face8731a4" :answer/id #uuid "23ebcbf3-35d1-5715-bd12-20face8731a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186200416 :answer-field/correct "answer-088c8755-232f-5d67-b714-cb5f32d8ba5e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-088c8755-232f-5d67-b714-cb5f32d8ba5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-088c8755-232f-5d67-b714-cb5f32d8ba5e" :answer/id #uuid "088c8755-232f-5d67-b714-cb5f32d8ba5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3db815a9-d784-52dd-bf7e-2dc644a57255" :question/id #uuid "3db815a9-d784-52dd-bf7e-2dc644a57255" :question/math-academy-id "q-213507" :question/problem "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5x+4≡23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+4 & ≡23\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x+4-4 & ≡23-4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡19\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡1\\cdot 13+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=5$ and $n=13$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(5,13)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To find the inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$, we compute the exponents of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡25≡1\\cdot 13+12≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡12\\cdot 5≡60≡4\\cdot 13+8≡8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ≡8\\cdot 5≡40≡3\\cdot 13+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $8\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ which means that $8$ is the multiplicative inverse of $5$ (modulo $13$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $8,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 8\\cdot 5x & ≡8\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ (8\\cdot 5)x & ≡48 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡9+3\\cdot 13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ x & ≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-92526c99-58f2-5c30-97c0-6d85b023cfa3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-92526c99-58f2-5c30-97c0-6d85b023cfa3" :answer-field/id #uuid "92526c99-58f2-5c30-97c0-6d85b023cfa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f6e32835-b8b0-5ef3-9fcc-e5ddf1c98c10" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5fd8304-33a3-582c-8a4f-dbb98a08be96" "answer-c0555944-5da1-5f24-9981-3b0e3a814a45" "answer-44378403-b1a6-51ab-bdb4-8a83dc56f909" "answer-a57931da-2515-5c40-8190-f20e4d544a5e" "answer-f6e32835-b8b0-5ef3-9fcc-e5ddf1c98c10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5fd8304-33a3-582c-8a4f-dbb98a08be96" :answer/id #uuid "e5fd8304-33a3-582c-8a4f-dbb98a08be96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0555944-5da1-5f24-9981-3b0e3a814a45" :answer/id #uuid "c0555944-5da1-5f24-9981-3b0e3a814a45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44378403-b1a6-51ab-bdb4-8a83dc56f909" :answer/id #uuid "44378403-b1a6-51ab-bdb4-8a83dc56f909" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a57931da-2515-5c40-8190-f20e4d544a5e" :answer/id #uuid "a57931da-2515-5c40-8190-f20e4d544a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6e32835-b8b0-5ef3-9fcc-e5ddf1c98c10" :answer/id #uuid "f6e32835-b8b0-5ef3-9fcc-e5ddf1c98c10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b2d85f9-70d5-5d8a-aa2a-9b3b30df44ef" :question/id #uuid "7b2d85f9-70d5-5d8a-aa2a-9b3b30df44ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-213522" :question/problem "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n4x+3≡34\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),\n$$\n\nexpressing your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $13.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & ≡34\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x+3-3 & ≡34-3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡31\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡2\\cdot 13+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=4$ and $n=13$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,13)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To find the inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$, we compute the exponents of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16≡1\\cdot 13+3≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{4} & ≡12\\cdot 4≡48≡3\\cdot 13+9≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{5} & ≡9\\cdot 4≡36≡2\\cdot 13+10≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{6} & ≡10\\cdot 4≡40≡3\\cdot 13+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $10\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ which means that $10$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $13$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $10,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 10\\cdot 4x & ≡10\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ (10\\cdot 4)x & ≡50 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡11+3\\cdot 13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ x & ≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-05b43649-64c4-5c3e-ae9c-72a41cf3ebb6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-05b43649-64c4-5c3e-ae9c-72a41cf3ebb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "05b43649-64c4-5c3e-ae9c-72a41cf3ebb6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e5ea6e5b-5c39-5cf4-a983-f88e1861ecd4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5ea6e5b-5c39-5cf4-a983-f88e1861ecd4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5ea6e5b-5c39-5cf4-a983-f88e1861ecd4" :answer/id #uuid "e5ea6e5b-5c39-5cf4-a983-f88e1861ecd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3b5eee82-5740-5d92-bedf-cc94230c0e60" :question/id #uuid "3b5eee82-5740-5d92-bedf-cc94230c0e60" :question/math-academy-id "q-132295" :question/problem "Solve the congruence $35x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=35$ and $n=51$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(35,51)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$ To find the inverse of $35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm.  \n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}51 & = & 35\\cdot 1 & + & 16 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 35 & = & 16\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 16 & = & 3\\cdot 5 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =51-35\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3 & =35-16\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =16-3\\cdot 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =16-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16-(35-16\\cdot 2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 11-35\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(51-35)\\cdot 11-35\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =51\\cdot 11-35\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =51\\cdot 11+35\\cdot (-16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n1=51\\cdot 11+35\\cdot (-16).\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $51,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51\\cdot 11+35\\cdot (-16) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot (-16) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot (51-16) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot 35 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $35\\cdot 35≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51),$ which means that \n$35$  is its own multiplicative inverse ( (modulo $51$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $35,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot 35x & ≡35\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ (35\\cdot 35)x & ≡175 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡3\\cdot 51+22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ x & ≡22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-32ef70e3-6162-577a-bcdf-82f0147aac4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-32ef70e3-6162-577a-bcdf-82f0147aac4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "32ef70e3-6162-577a-bcdf-82f0147aac4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-67b9f08f-ccc6-5559-9c89-4b109a747d41" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67b9f08f-ccc6-5559-9c89-4b109a747d41" "answer-882df7c4-3a86-5d50-8e6d-f1ec40ab280a" "answer-69e7f375-a389-5bdc-89d6-4d38cccfbe8c" "answer-7ddb8243-ef75-5cc7-9d5f-07daa908cd86" "answer-fb1c70ff-3515-5c02-9286-ee2663506546"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67b9f08f-ccc6-5559-9c89-4b109a747d41" :answer/id #uuid "67b9f08f-ccc6-5559-9c89-4b109a747d41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-882df7c4-3a86-5d50-8e6d-f1ec40ab280a" :answer/id #uuid "882df7c4-3a86-5d50-8e6d-f1ec40ab280a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡25\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69e7f375-a389-5bdc-89d6-4d38cccfbe8c" :answer/id #uuid "69e7f375-a389-5bdc-89d6-4d38cccfbe8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡32\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ddb8243-ef75-5cc7-9d5f-07daa908cd86" :answer/id #uuid "7ddb8243-ef75-5cc7-9d5f-07daa908cd86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡40\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb1c70ff-3515-5c02-9286-ee2663506546" :answer/id #uuid "fb1c70ff-3515-5c02-9286-ee2663506546" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡43\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6fe75766-c260-5668-9726-1bdf3c8f2f17" :question/id #uuid "6fe75766-c260-5668-9726-1bdf3c8f2f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-78069" :question/problem "Solve the congruence $15x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=43$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,43)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$ To find the inverse of $15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm. \n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}43 & = & 15\\cdot 2 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 13\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 13 & = & 2\\cdot 6 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =43-15\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2 & =15-13 \\\\ 1 & =13-2\\cdot 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =13-2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =13-(15-13)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =(43-15\\cdot 2)\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n1=43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20).\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $43,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot (-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot (43-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot 23 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $15\\cdot 23≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43),$ which means that $23$ is the multiplicative inverse of $15$ (modulo $43$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $23,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 23\\cdot 15x & ≡23\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ (23\\cdot 15)x & ≡92 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡92 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b72cedeb-72f7-5d00-aef1-922a6cd3715e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b72cedeb-72f7-5d00-aef1-922a6cd3715e" :answer-field/id #uuid "b72cedeb-72f7-5d00-aef1-922a6cd3715e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-925492cf-b212-5f24-a956-5e4ebd50534c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44efd338-666a-5ada-a785-765da28084d0" "answer-d256f5a7-e3af-5e6b-8f30-286d8ceacb40" "answer-f4efd977-d8da-5378-8891-6af2bd5f45d7" "answer-b19af155-55c5-534a-b966-b4cb8b712651" "answer-925492cf-b212-5f24-a956-5e4ebd50534c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44efd338-666a-5ada-a785-765da28084d0" :answer/id #uuid "44efd338-666a-5ada-a785-765da28084d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d256f5a7-e3af-5e6b-8f30-286d8ceacb40" :answer/id #uuid "d256f5a7-e3af-5e6b-8f30-286d8ceacb40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡17\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4efd977-d8da-5378-8891-6af2bd5f45d7" :answer/id #uuid "f4efd977-d8da-5378-8891-6af2bd5f45d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b19af155-55c5-534a-b966-b4cb8b712651" :answer/id #uuid "b19af155-55c5-534a-b966-b4cb8b712651" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡34\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-925492cf-b212-5f24-a956-5e4ebd50534c" :answer/id #uuid "925492cf-b212-5f24-a956-5e4ebd50534c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205227 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following linear congruence:\n\n$$\n4x \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\nSince the modulus $n=5$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error.   \n\nSubstituting the trial solutions $x = 0,1,2,3,4$ into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 4\\cdot 1 & ≡4≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 4\\cdot 2 & ≡8≡5+3≡3≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 4\\cdot 3 & ≡12≡2\\cdot 5+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & ✓ \\\\ 4\\cdot 4 & ≡16≡3\\cdot 5+1≡1≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv  {\\color{blue}{3}} \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $3$ modulo $5$ is a solution. For example, note that $8 \\equiv 3,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡4\\cdot 8\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡32\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 5+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡2\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205228 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider the following linear congruence:\n\n$$\n4x \\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:21)\n$$\n\nWe can solve this congruence by multiplying both sides by the *multiplicative inverse* of $4$ modulo $21.$ \n\nIn this case, we can find the multiplicative inverse by raising $4$ to positive integer exponents until we get an answer of $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡4\\cdot 16≡64≡3\\cdot 21+1≡3\\cdot 0+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $4 \\cdot {\\color{blue}{16}} \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:21),$ which means that $16$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $21$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $16,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 16\\cdot 4x & ≡16\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ (16\\cdot 4)x & ≡80 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡3\\cdot 21+17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ x & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 17 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:21).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205229 :tutorial/content "Not every linear congruence has a unique solution! \n\nTo determine whether a linear congruence has a single unique solution, we can use the following rule:\n\n*A linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$ *\n\nWe'll prove this theorem at the end of the lesson.\n\nSo, whenever we solve a linear congruence, we should first use the above rule to check that there is a unique solution. In the linear congruence $4x \\equiv 5 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:21),$ we had $a=4$ and $n=21.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,21)=1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that our congruence does indeed have a unique solution. \n\nIn a future lesson, we'll discuss cases where linear congruences have multiple or no solutions." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205230 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following theorem:\n\n*Let $a$, $b,$ and $n$ integers. If $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$, then the linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$  has a unique solution.*\n\n**Proof**.  \n\nTo prove this theorem, we must show that a solution exists and that it is unique. \n\nFirst, we prove that a solution exists:\n\n- If $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$, then by Bézout's identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that \n\n$$\nau+nv=1.\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}au+nv & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ au & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis means that $u$ is the multiplicative inverse of $a$ (modulo $n$).\n\nSo, multiplying both sides of the original congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ by $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ u\\cdot ax & ≡u\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ (u\\cdot a)x & ≡ub &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡ub &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ x & ≡ub &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv ub \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$ is a solution.\n\nNext, we prove the solution is unique:\n\n- To prove uniqueness, we show that two solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ to our congruence must be congruent. \n\nSuppose $x_1$ and $x_2$ are two solutions to the congruence, i.e., $ax_1 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$ and $ax_2 \\equiv b\\:  (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$. We want to show that $x_1 \\equiv x_2$.\n\nSince $ax_1 \\equiv b\\:  (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$ and $ax_2 \\equiv b\\:  (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$, we have\n\n$$\nax_1 \\equiv ax_2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\text{gcd}(a,n) = 1$, we can multiply both sides by the modular inverse of $a$ modulo $n ,$ which we previously denoted $u.$ \n\n$$\n(u \\cdot a) x_1 \\equiv (u \\cdot a) x_2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})\n$$\n\nSince $a$ and $n$ are relatively prime, $u$ exists. Therefore, $u \\cdot a \\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$, and we have\n\n$$\nx_1 \\equiv x_2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n}),\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThus, we have shown both the existence and uniqueness of the solution to the congruence $ax \\equiv b \\pmod{n}$ when $\\text{gcd}(a,n) = 1$." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204121 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
